L["cana"] = """Three days after Jesus called his first disciples, there was a wedding in Cana, a village in Galilee. Jesus' mother Mary was there, and Jesus and his disciples had also been invited. In those days, a wedding was a community celebration that could last for days. Running out of wine would have been a serious embarrassment for the host, and it could haunt a young couple for years.

Partway through the feast, the wine ran out. Mary noticed and went to Jesus. They have no more wine, she said. Jesus answered, woman, why do you involve me? My hour has not yet come. His time to be revealed publicly had not yet arrived. But Mary didn't argue. She turned to the servants and said, do whatever he tells you.

Nearby stood six stone water jars, the kind used for ceremonial washing. Each held twenty to thirty gallons. Jesus said to the servants, fill the jars with water. They filled them to the brim. Then he told them, now draw some out and take it to the master of the banquet. They did.

The master of the banquet tasted the water that had been turned into wine. He didn't know where it had come from, though the servants who had drawn the water knew. Then he called the bridegroom aside. Everyone brings out the choice wine first and then the cheaper wine after the guests have had too much to drink. But you have saved the best till now.

The amount was staggering, around a hundred and twenty to a hundred and eighty gallons of excellent wine. Jesus didn't just solve the problem. He overflowed it.

John tells us that this, the first of his signs, was performed by Jesus in Cana of Galilee. He thus revealed his glory, and his disciples believed in him.

Why does this matter? John calls the miracles signs, because they point to something. Water that was used for ritual washing became wine for a celebration. Jesus was showing that he came to bring joy that the old rituals couldn't. The prophets often pictured the coming kingdom as a feast with the best wine. Jesus began his work at a party.

What happened after? From Cana, Jesus went to Capernaum, and then to Jerusalem for Passover. His mother appears again later in John's gospel, standing near the cross. Her advice at the wedding was the last thing she is recorded as saying in this gospel. Do whatever he tells you.

What does this mean for us today? Jesus cares about ordinary needs, even a social embarrassment at a wedding. Mary didn't give him instructions. She just told him the problem and trusted him. It also shows what obedience looks like. The servants did something that seemed pointless, filling jars with water, and they were the ones who saw the miracle. And Jesus often gives more than we ask for.

That is why this story is so important. It shows that Jesus has power over creation, and it reveals his glory at the very start. It also points ahead to the greater feast, when Jesus gave his disciples a cup and spoke of the new covenant in his blood, and to the wedding supper of the Lamb at the end of time."""
